---
/**
 * DETAIL ROUTE — /industries/[slug]
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Generated from the shared data files, so adding a category creates its route
 * automatically — no new page file needed.
 *
 * TWO KINDS OF PAGE, ONE ROUTE
 *   A slug with a written page renders it (`IndustryDetailPage`); a slug
 *   without one renders the reservation shell (`ReservationPage`). The branch is
 *   driven by `industryPageFor()`, so building a page for real is: write its
 *   data file, add it to `industryDetailPages` in `src/data/industry-detail.js`,
 *   and let the path be subtracted from `detailRoutePaths` — which that
 *   registry's `builtIndustryPaths` does automatically, so the noindex entry and
 *   the sitemap entry can never disagree.
 *
 *   As of the nine-category brief all nine slugs have a page file, so the
 *   reservation branch now only guards a future tenth category. It is kept
 *   rather than deleted for exactly that reason: the routes were published
 *   before the content existed and every link to them is live, so a category
 *   added to `industries.js` must resolve to something real the moment it is
 *   added.
 *
 * WHY NOT ONE FILE PER CATEGORY
 *   A static `src/pages/industries/<slug>.astro` would also work, but then the
 *   route list lives in two places (the file system and the data registry) and
 *   the reservation fallback has to be duplicated for every category still to
 *   be written. One route, two branches, one registry — the same architecture
 *   `src/pages/services/[slug].astro` uses.
 *
 * THE COST OF ONE ROUTE FOR TWO BRANCHES — a stated trade-off, not an oversight
 *   Because both shells are imported statically, Astro links BOTH branches'
 *   stylesheets on EVERY `/industries/<slug>` page, so a built category page
 *   ships `inner-pages.css` (the reservation shell's sheet) without using it.
 *   That is ~1 KB gzipped of CSS the visitor has almost certainly downloaded
 *   already — `inner-pages.css` is also the sheet for `/industries`,
 *   `/insights` and `/case-studies`. The alternative is the same path in two
 *   route tables, where Astro's static-over-dynamic precedence would decide the
 *   winner silently. The trade-off is the same one the services route already
 *   documents and accepts.
 *
 *   SECOND, SMALLER COST, MEASURED: because these pages import the same shared
 *   sheets as `/industries`, `/industries.html` links to differently-named CSS
 *   chunks than it did before these routes existed — the bundler recomputes
 *   which modules go in which chunk once a sheet has two entry points. The page's
 *   markup is byte-identical once those hrefs are normalised; only the asset
 *   file names change. Nothing to fix, worth knowing before diffing a build.
 *
 * INDEXABILITY
 *   The reservation branch is `noindex` and absent from sitemap.xml. A built
 *   page is indexable — its path is subtracted from `detailRoutePaths` in
 *   `src/data/routes.js`, which is what drives both the `<meta name="robots">`
 *   split and the sitemap filter. Forgetting that subtraction leaves a finished,
 *   content-rich page excluded from search; `npm run check` cross-checks the two
 *   signals and fails the build if a page is noindex but listed, or indexable
 *   but absent.
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 */
import ReservationPage from '../../components/ReservationPage.astro';
import IndustryDetailPage from '../../components/industries/IndustryDetailPage.astro';

import { industryPageFor } from '../../data/industry-detail.js';
import { detailRouteGroups, reservationForDetail } from '../../data/routes.js';

export function getStaticPaths() {
  return detailRouteGroups['industries'].map((entry) => {
    const detail = industryPageFor(entry.slug);

    return {
      params: { slug: entry.slug },
      props: detail
        ? { detail }
        : {
            detail: null,
            page: reservationForDetail(entry.kind, entry.item, entry.parent),
          },
    };
  });
}

const { detail, page } = Astro.props;
---

{detail ? <IndustryDetailPage page={detail} /> : <ReservationPage {...page} />}
